\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The waveform gallery: every channel's radiation, one row per scenario, one retarded clock. Left column: $r\,\mathrm  {Re}\,\Psi _4$ of the dominant mode at the innermost extraction sphere, each row on its own stated scale---the lone throat radiates an order of magnitude below the mergers, and the fly-by is the loudest channel of all. Half-height caps mark record ends: the collapsing throat's stream is junk-gated at $t=70$, the spiral record ends at $t=60$ with the run still in flight, and the fly-by---complete to $t=100$, no horizon ever forming---is gated at $t=76$, where the mouths' expansion (areal radii growing $\times 7.8$) lifts the $R=20$ sphere off its post-burst trough. Right column: the envelope $|r\Psi _4|$ at every extraction sphere on its own retarded time (ink to faint, inner to outer): a signal travelling at $c$ and falling as $1/r$ folds onto one curve. Burgundy dots mark the correlated wavefront, and the quoted $v/c$ is the lag of the whole complex waveform between neighbouring spheres over their common retarded window---peak-to-peak timing fails on flat-topped envelopes (the vacuum twin's merger plateau is ${\sim }15$ units wide at both spheres, and its peak times alone would read $v=0.57c$ where the waveform lag reads $0.82c$). Measured speeds: throat $0.91/0.95/0.98$, head-on $0.96/0.90$, spiral $0.95/1.00/1.00$, fly-by $1.00/1.00/1.00$, vacuum twin $0.82$. Every channel propagates at $c$ within the output cadence: radiation, not a gauge or constraint mode.}}{10}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gw_gallery}{{6}{10}{The waveform gallery: every channel's radiation, one row per scenario, one retarded clock. Left column: $r\,\mathrm {Re}\,\Psi _4$ of the dominant mode at the innermost extraction sphere, each row on its own stated scale---the lone throat radiates an order of magnitude below the mergers, and the fly-by is the loudest channel of all. Half-height caps mark record ends: the collapsing throat's stream is junk-gated at $t=70$, the spiral record ends at $t=60$ with the run still in flight, and the fly-by---complete to $t=100$, no horizon ever forming---is gated at $t=76$, where the mouths' expansion (areal radii growing $\times 7.8$) lifts the $R=20$ sphere off its post-burst trough. Right column: the envelope $|r\Psi _4|$ at every extraction sphere on its own retarded time (ink to faint, inner to outer): a signal travelling at $c$ and falling as $1/r$ folds onto one curve. Burgundy dots mark the correlated wavefront, and the quoted $v/c$ is the lag of the whole complex waveform between neighbouring spheres over their common retarded window---peak-to-peak timing fails on flat-topped envelopes (the vacuum twin's merger plateau is ${\sim }15$ units wide at both spheres, and its peak times alone would read $v=0.57c$ where the waveform lag reads $0.82c$). Measured speeds: throat $0.91/0.95/0.98$, head-on $0.96/0.90$, spiral $0.95/1.00/1.00$, fly-by $1.00/1.00/1.00$, vacuum twin $0.82$. Every channel propagates at $c$ within the output cadence: radiation, not a gauge or constraint mode}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Every source on a detector, at one shared calibration: total mass $M=30\,M_\odot $, distance $D=10$ Mpc; strain scales as $M/D$ and frequency as $1/M$. Each record is reduced to units of its own total ADM mass first---$2$ code units for the four binaries, $1$ for the lone throat---so that one calibration means the same thing for all five. (a)~Envelopes $|r\Psi _4|M$ of the innermost-sphere records on a common merger clock, $\tau =0$ at each record's own peak: the fly-by is the loudest channel ($8.2\times 10^{-2}$), then the spiral ($5.9\times 10^{-2}$), the head-on ($4.7\times 10^{-2}$), the vacuum twin ($2.2\times 10^{-2}$) and the lone throat ($3.1\times 10^{-3}$). (b)~Strain amplitude spectral densities over the aLIGO design floor (burgundy). The fly-by peaks at $6.0\times 10^{-21}\,\mathrm  {Hz}^{-1/2}$ ($178$ Hz); the three mergers lie within a factor of $2.4$ of one another ($1.2$--$2.9\times 10^{-21}$, at $90$--$135$ Hz); the lone collapsing throat sits an order below ($3.9\times 10^{-22}$). The faint straight line is the analytic Newtonian inspiral of the same equal-mass binary, $|\tilde  h(f)|\propto \mathcal  {M}^{5/6}f^{-7/6}$, drawn to its ISCO at $147$ Hz through the same normalisation as the numerical record: the ramp an astrophysical binary rides into the band where the simulated signal begins. (c)~Instantaneous frequency of each record---the energy-weighted derivative of the analytic signal's phase, drawn over the record's body ($|r\Psi _4|$ above $0.25$ of its peak)---against the Newtonian point-mass chirp of the same binary coalescing at $\tau =0$ (burgundy), with the ISCO ($147$ Hz) and the $\ell =m=2$ ringdown of the equal-mass remnant ($599$ Hz) as rules. The vacuum twin is the control that fixes the scale: its peak sits at $fM=0.065$, the textbook merger value, and it climbs $299\to 473$ Hz along the point-mass chirp before peeling off at merger. No wormhole channel does: the head-on bell falls $486\to 342$ Hz, the collapsing throat rings at $335$ Hz at its peak ($fM=0.050$, the $20.6\,M$ period of Sec.~\ref {sec:single:gw}), the fly-by falls $218\to 66$ Hz as the pair separates, and the spiral (record in flight) climbs $151\to 679$ Hz without settling onto a ringdown. (d)~Energy radiated in the dominant multipole, $E_{\rm  rad}/M$, ranked; the ink tick on each bar is the spread over that arm's extraction spheres, which is the error bar that catches near-zone content posing as a wave. The two open burgundy bars are not runs of this campaign: they are the published equal-mass non-spinning vacuum results from rest at infinity---head-on infall, $5.5\times 10^{-4}$, and quasi-circular inspiral-merger, $1-M_f/M=4.84\times 10^{-2}$---between which any vacuum binary of this mass ratio must fall. The vacuum twin ($2.6\times 10^{-3}$) sits just above the head-on end, as a plunge launched with $59\%$ of the circular momentum should; the spiral ($2.2\times 10^{-2}$) and the fly-by ($9.3\times 10^{-2}$) outradiate it by $8.5\times $ and $36\times $, the fly-by by more than a full quasi-circular black-hole merger. Energies are the band integral of $|\DOTSI \intop \ilimits@ r\Psi _4\,\mathrm  {d}t'|^2$ over a common retarded window, doubled for the $\pm m$ pair; each is flat against the low-frequency cut over $0.02$--$0.45$ of its own spectral peak. This is the gravitational channel only: the scalar carries its own energy, which $\Psi _4$ does not see.}}{11}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gw_ligo}{{7}{11}{Every source on a detector, at one shared calibration: total mass $M=30\,M_\odot $, distance $D=10$ Mpc; strain scales as $M/D$ and frequency as $1/M$. Each record is reduced to units of its own total ADM mass first---$2$ code units for the four binaries, $1$ for the lone throat---so that one calibration means the same thing for all five. (a)~Envelopes $|r\Psi _4|M$ of the innermost-sphere records on a common merger clock, $\tau =0$ at each record's own peak: the fly-by is the loudest channel ($8.2\times 10^{-2}$), then the spiral ($5.9\times 10^{-2}$), the head-on ($4.7\times 10^{-2}$), the vacuum twin ($2.2\times 10^{-2}$) and the lone throat ($3.1\times 10^{-3}$). (b)~Strain amplitude spectral densities over the aLIGO design floor (burgundy). The fly-by peaks at $6.0\times 10^{-21}\,\mathrm {Hz}^{-1/2}$ ($178$ Hz); the three mergers lie within a factor of $2.4$ of one another ($1.2$--$2.9\times 10^{-21}$, at $90$--$135$ Hz); the lone collapsing throat sits an order below ($3.9\times 10^{-22}$). The faint straight line is the analytic Newtonian inspiral of the same equal-mass binary, $|\tilde h(f)|\propto \mathcal {M}^{5/6}f^{-7/6}$, drawn to its ISCO at $147$ Hz through the same normalisation as the numerical record: the ramp an astrophysical binary rides into the band where the simulated signal begins. (c)~Instantaneous frequency of each record---the energy-weighted derivative of the analytic signal's phase, drawn over the record's body ($|r\Psi _4|$ above $0.25$ of its peak)---against the Newtonian point-mass chirp of the same binary coalescing at $\tau =0$ (burgundy), with the ISCO ($147$ Hz) and the $\ell =m=2$ ringdown of the equal-mass remnant ($599$ Hz) as rules. The vacuum twin is the control that fixes the scale: its peak sits at $fM=0.065$, the textbook merger value, and it climbs $299\to 473$ Hz along the point-mass chirp before peeling off at merger. No wormhole channel does: the head-on bell falls $486\to 342$ Hz, the collapsing throat rings at $335$ Hz at its peak ($fM=0.050$, the $20.6\,M$ period of Sec.~\ref {sec:single:gw}), the fly-by falls $218\to 66$ Hz as the pair separates, and the spiral (record in flight) climbs $151\to 679$ Hz without settling onto a ringdown. (d)~Energy radiated in the dominant multipole, $E_{\rm rad}/M$, ranked; the ink tick on each bar is the spread over that arm's extraction spheres, which is the error bar that catches near-zone content posing as a wave. The two open burgundy bars are not runs of this campaign: they are the published equal-mass non-spinning vacuum results from rest at infinity---head-on infall, $5.5\times 10^{-4}$, and quasi-circular inspiral-merger, $1-M_f/M=4.84\times 10^{-2}$---between which any vacuum binary of this mass ratio must fall. The vacuum twin ($2.6\times 10^{-3}$) sits just above the head-on end, as a plunge launched with $59\%$ of the circular momentum should; the spiral ($2.2\times 10^{-2}$) and the fly-by ($9.3\times 10^{-2}$) outradiate it by $8.5\times $ and $36\times $, the fly-by by more than a full quasi-circular black-hole merger. Energies are the band integral of $|\int r\Psi _4\,\mathrm {d}t'|^2$ over a common retarded window, doubled for the $\pm m$ pair; each is flat against the low-frequency cut over $0.02$--$0.45$ of its own spectral peak. This is the gravitational channel only: the scalar carries its own energy, which $\Psi _4$ does not see}{figure.7}{}}
